.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH mdrun 1 2026-11-16 "mdrun 0.1.0" "mdrun manual"
.SH NAME
mdrun \- Create, run, and verify executable Markdown documents.
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmdrun\fR [\fB\-\-workdir\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] <\fIsubcommands\fR>
.SH DESCRIPTION
Create, run, and verify executable Markdown documents.
.PP
Documents combine Markdown notes, executable code, and recorded output. Omit note text, command code, or image input to read it from stdin.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-workdir\fR \fI<DIR>\fR
Execute code in DIR while resolving document paths from the current directory
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
mdrun\-init(1)
Create a document with a title, timestamp, and unique identifier
.TP
mdrun\-note(1)
Append a Markdown note to a document
.TP
mdrun\-exec(1)
Execute code and append its combined stdout and stderr
.TP
mdrun\-image(1)
Copy an image and append its Markdown reference
.TP
mdrun\-pop(1)
Remove the most recent note or code/output pair
.TP
mdrun\-verify(1)
Replay executable blocks and report changed output
.TP
mdrun\-verify\-many(1)
Verify independent documents with bounded cooperative sessions
.TP
mdrun\-extract(1)
Print shell commands that recreate a document
.TP
mdrun\-list(1)
List executable blocks without running commands
.TP
mdrun\-preview(1)
Print one executable block\*(Aqs complete fenced source without running it
.TP
mdrun\-session(1)
Run an author command with one managed application binding
.TP
mdrun\-guide(1)
Read a focused guide offline, or list the available topics
.SH EXTRA
Examples:
  Record a command and check its output again:
    mdrun init demo.md \*(AqA repeatable greeting\*(Aq
    mdrun note demo.md \*(AqThis command prints a greeting.\*(Aq
    mdrun exec demo.md sh \*(Aqprintf "hello from mdrun\\n"\*(Aq
    mdrun verify demo.md

Inspection:
  list and preview read documents without executing their commands.
  verify executes commands and compares recorded output; it does not undo effects.

For one command\*(Aqs syntax and examples, use mdrun COMMAND \-\-help.
For longer authoring workflows, use mdrun guide documents.
For cooperative warm execution and binding requirements, use mdrun guide sessions.
For exit statuses, files and environment, use mdrun guide reference.
.SH VERSION
v0.1.0
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\&Successful help, version, guide, document editing, inventory or preview
\&\fB0\fR
.TP
\&\fBverify\fR: recorded output matches
\&\fB0\fR
.TP
\&\fBverify\fR: changed recorded output or execution/document error
\&\fB1\fR
.TP
\&Invalid CLI usage
\&\fB2\fR from clap
.TP
\&\fBexec\fR
\&Executed program's exit status; \fB255\fR for signal termination on supported Unix platforms
.TP
\&\fBsession\fR
\&Author's exit status, or \fB128 + signal\fR for a signalled author, when session health/cleanup succeeds; \fB1\fR for infrastructure or uncertain cleanup failure
.TP
\&Forwarded binding executable
\&Application status, including native signal termination for a signalled cold process; \fB125\fR on forwarding/control failure
.TP
\&\fBverify\-many\fR
\&\fB0\fR only when all requested documents and batch cleanup succeed; otherwise \fB1\fR
.PP
\&An executed program may itself return \fB1\fR, \fB2\fR or \fB125\fR; interpret the status in the context of the command you ran. \fBexec\fR records output even for nonzero status. \fBverify\fR checks output, not application exit status, and does not undo side effects. Managed\-session reports distinguish application results from infrastructure and cleanup failures; use \fBmdrun guide sessions\fR for version 1 field meanings and cold\-reason keys.
.SH INPUT AND OUTPUT
.PP
\&\fBnote\fR, \fBexec\fR and \fBimage\fR read their omitted text/code/image argument from stdin. Executed document blocks read EOF from \fB/dev/null\fR on stdin unless their own code supplies input. \fBexec\fR merges stdout/stderr into one capture and prints that capture on stdout. Verification diffs, inventory, previews, extraction and guide text use stdout; mdrun diagnostics use stderr.
.PP
\&Session invocation streams preserve separate stdout/stderr unless the caller merges them. Application output must use the adapter's request\-owned streams. Session and batch reports go to \fB\-\-report FILE\fR and contain metadata, not captured application bytes or credentials. Human output is not a machine schema: use \fBlist \-\-json\fR and the versioned reports for automation.
.SH FILES
.IP \(bu 2
\&Document/image/output/report paths resolve from the caller's current directory. \fB\-\-workdir DIR\fR affects executed programs, not those path arguments.
.IP \(bu 2
\&\fBinit\fR refuses an existing destination. \fBnote\fR, \fBexec\fR, \fBimage\fR and \fBpop\fR edit the selected document.
.IP \(bu 2
\&\fBverify\fR leaves its input unchanged unless that same path is explicitly selected with \fB\-\-output\fR. An output copy may replace an existing destination, so choose its path deliberately.
.IP \(bu 2
\&Images are copied beside the document. Removing their Markdown entry does not remove the copied file.
.IP \(bu 2
\&Binding files are supplied explicitly; mdrun does not search ancestor directories for a binding.
.IP \(bu 2
\&Report paths must not alias the binding, a verification input or its selected output, including through symlinks or hard links. Complete reports replace their destination through a temporary file and rename; a publication error fails the command.
.IP \(bu 2
\&Managed sessions create private temporary state with protected credentials. Successful cleanup removes it; uncertain cleanup can retain state and report its status path.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\&\fBPATH\fR
\&Finds document interpreters and ordinary programs. Managed sessions prepend only their explicit forwarded executable binding.
.TP
\&\fBSHOWBOAT_REMOTE_URL\fR
\&Optional compatible remote receiver. Editing commands POST document updates; unset/empty disables delivery. Delivery failure warns without discarding the local edit.
.TP
\&\fBMDRUN_SESSION\fR
\&Internal managed\-session context, set by mdrun for its children. Do not set it manually or copy private configuration into documents.
.PP
\&The invoked application receives its execution environment. Warm admission compares context; runtime startup variables may require cold execution according to the binding. The host needs no installed Rust, Go, JVM, Node or Python runtime to start, but commands/adapters still need their own tools.
.SH REMOTE DELIVERY
.PP
\&Remote updates retain the compatible \fBSHOWBOAT_REMOTE_URL\fR name. \fBinit\fR, \fBnote\fR, \fBexec\fR and \fBpop\fR send URL\-encoded form data; \fBimage\fR sends multipart data. The document UUID identifies the stream. Network errors are warnings rather than transaction rollback; local document success does not prove remote delivery.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
.PP
\&Existing Showboat document provenance remains intact. New documents identify mdrun. Source\-preserving edits intentionally retain unmanaged spacing and line endings. Clap owns normal help, errors, option handling and \fB\-\-\fR; incidental Showboat parser quirks are not compatibility requirements.
